      "restoredAt": null,
      "sourceFeatureExtractionItemID": null,
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "I agree with this opinion. Since it is “Tracked”, I very much hope it will be implemented in the not-too-distant future.\n\nVarious opinions on relationships have been posted, but I believe that measures to address the entry point of the problem are the least costly and most effective, and I would like to see them implemented.\n\nPlease allow me to make a specific proposal to help lower the cost of consideration.\n\nThe following images are social as of 2025/04/08.\n\nI would guess that the area I have circled in yellow marker is the current composition of the block elements. (The area I have highlighted in blue is my guess for the inline elements.)\n\nWhat I would suggest is to add a block element at the bottom with the same shape as the top block element. And the inline elements should be canned. (e.g., This instance contains a blocked or blocked user.)\n\nSince the code to determine the presence of a “friend” user in an instance already exists in the social implementation, there is no need to create new code to determine the presence of a “blocked” user in an instance. Also, there is no need to retrieve and display user data. Just display a canned message.\n\nImaginary code\n\n```\n\ncurrent_world = get_current_world(friend)\n\nusers_in_world = get_users_in_world(current_world)\n\nhas_blocked_user = users_in_world.any? { |user| user[:blocked] }\n\nif has_blocked_user\n\n  # Add block elements to display\n\nend\n\n```",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
